<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedict Biscop</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 628–<a href="/years/690">690</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benedict Biscop (c. 628–690) was a Northumbrian nobleman who became a monk and founded the monasteries of Wearmouth in 674 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> around 682. On repeated journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he collected books, relics and paintings, and he brought masons and glaziers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> to build in stone. The library he assembled made possible the scholarship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, who wrote his life.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benedict Biscop, also known as Biscop Baducing, was born into a noble Northumbrian family and served for a time as a thegn of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> of Bernicia. At about the age of 25, around 653, he set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>. Wilfrid was detained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and Benedict completed the journey alone; on his return he was said to be full of fervour for the good of the English Church. It has been suggested that he is the Biscop Beding named in the Anglian Collection of royal genealogies as a son of Beda Bubbing, King of Mercia.</p><p>A second journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> followed about twelve years later. Alchfrith of Deira, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>, wished to go with him, but the king refused permission. On the way home Benedict stayed at the island monastery of Lérins off the coast of Provence from 665 to 667, where he took monastic vows and the name Benedict. On a third visit Pope Vitalian asked him to accompany Archbishop Theodore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> in 669, and Theodore made him abbot of the monastery of SS. Peter and Paul at Canterbury for two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a> granted him land for a monastery in 674. Benedict recruited masons on the Continent able to build in the Pre-Romanesque style, and on his last journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 679 he brought back books, relics, stonemasons, glaziers and a grant of privileges from Pope Agatho. The monastery was the first church building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> constructed in stone, and its glass was a novelty in 7th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 682 he named Eosterwine as his coadjutor, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> he set Ceolfrid over the sister house of Saint Paul, who took 20 monks from Wearmouth to found it.</p><p>He drew up a rule for the community based on that of Saint Benedict and on the customs of seventeen monasteries he had visited, and he engaged Abbot John, arch-cantor of Saint Peter's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, to teach Roman chant. His library, mostly service books but also scripture and classical works, is estimated at about 250 titles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> copied there were prized across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, the earliest surviving complete Latin Vulgate Bible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Benedict was bedridden for the last three years of his life and died on 12 January 690. A sermon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> points to an early cult, which spread more widely after his relics were moved to Thorney under Ethelwold around 980. The Catholic Church, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> keep his feast on 12 January, and the parish church of Wombourne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> is the only one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> dedicated to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benedict Biscop” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Benedict Biscop</b> founded Wearmouth in 674 and Jarrow in the early 680s, with books, glaziers and masons brought from Gaul and Rome, and Abbess Hild ruled the double monastery at Whitby.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Northumbria. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Monkwearmouth-Jarrow Abbey</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.91,-1.38&amp;year=668" aria-label="Monkwearmouth-Jarrow Abbey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4520 286 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Benedict Biscop"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4970 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=54.91,-1.38&amp;year=668">What was here in 668 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
